From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rdoupe <1207041+rdoupe@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] platform/x86: asus-wmi: restore thermal policy and fan curves
 after hibernation

Resume from hibernation follows a full power cycle. The firmware boots
again and the EC starts from its own default thermal policy and fan
tables, but the restored kernel still holds throttle_thermal_policy_mode
and the custom fan curves that were in force before hibernation.
asus_hotk_restore() refreshes rfkill, the keyboard backlight, fn-lock
and tablet mode, but never sends either of these back, so the platform
profile keeps reading e.g. "quiet" while the EC runs another table.

On an ASUS ROG Zephyrus G16 (GA605KM, BIOS 307) on battery, with the
asus-wmi profile at quiet, this left the three fans at about
2100/1800/3800 rpm with the APU idle at 28 C after a day of
suspend-then-hibernate cycles. The quiet table keeps them stopped below
about 54 C. Writing the profile again brought the quiet table back.

Write the cached policy back in .restore, then re-enable the custom fan
curves that were enabled before, since a policy write clears them.
Suspend to idle is not affected and is left alone: the EC keeps its
state there.

Downstream carry for omarchy-asus. Not yet submitted upstream.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/platform/x86/asus-wmi.c | 36 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/platform/x86/asus-wmi.c b/drivers/platform/x86/asus-wmi.c
--- a/drivers/platform/x86/asus-wmi.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/asus-wmi.c
@@ -5313,6 +5313,40 @@ static void asus_wmi_remove(struct platform_device *device)
 
 /* Platform driver - hibernate/resume callbacks *******************************/
 
+/*
+ * Resume from hibernation follows a full power cycle: the firmware boots and
+ * the EC starts from its own default thermal policy and fan tables, while the
+ * restored image still holds the mode and custom fan curves set before
+ * hibernation. Nothing re-sends them, so the platform profile reads e.g.
+ * "quiet" while the EC runs another table. Write them back.
+ */
+static void asus_wmi_restore_thermal_state(struct asus_wmi *asus)
+{
+	bool enabled[ARRAY_SIZE(asus->custom_fan_curves)];
+	int err, i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(asus->custom_fan_curves); i++)
+		enabled[i] = asus->custom_fan_curves[i].enabled;
+
+	if (asus->throttle_thermal_policy_dev) {
+		/* Clears the custom fan curves' enabled flags on success */
+		err = throttle_thermal_policy_write(asus);
+		if (err)
+			pr_warn("Failed to restore throttle thermal policy: %d\n", err);
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(asus->custom_fan_curves); i++) {
+		if (!enabled[i])
+			continue;
+		asus->custom_fan_curves[i].enabled = true;
+		err = fan_curve_write(asus, &asus->custom_fan_curves[i]);
+		if (err) {
+			pr_warn("Failed to restore custom fan curve %d: %d\n", i, err);
+			asus->custom_fan_curves[i].enabled = false;
+		}
+	}
+}
+
 static int asus_hotk_thaw(struct device *device)
 {
 	struct asus_wmi *asus = dev_get_drvdata(device);
@@ -5390,6 +5424,8 @@ static int asus_hotk_restore(struct device *device)
 	if (asus_wmi_has_fnlock_key(asus))
 		asus_wmi_fnlock_update(asus);
 
+	asus_wmi_restore_thermal_state(asus);
+
 	asus_wmi_tablet_mode_get_state(asus);
 	return 0;
 }
